  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nShowers and a few thunderstorms remain over portions of central and \nsoutheast IL which resides in a relatively stationary frontal zone. \nWith a complex of strong thunderstorms that pushed eastward along \nroughly the I-74 corridor this morning, an outflow pushed southward \nacross much of the area with cooler air and cloud cover tempering \ninstability across much of the area. However, SPC mesoanalysis \nindicates strong MLCAPE around 4000 J/KG lingering south of I-70. \nAnother shortwave impulse currently initiating thunderstorms near \nwestern portions of I-72 is showing effective bulk shear of 40-50 \nkts and could drive another complex of severe storms as moves over \nthat area this evening. SPC maintains a Slight Risk for severe \nstorms in east-central and southeast IL, with damaging wind as the \nhighest probability for severe weather (15% risk), but also a 2% \ntornado risk and 5% hail risk.\n\nTonight, most models have a relative lull in thunderstorm activity \ndespite a few storms developing over the stalled frontal boundary, \nhowever another nocturnal MCS looks to be moving across IA \novernight, heading toward northern or central IL Thursday morning. \nHigh-res ensembles depict 1000-200 J/kg MUCAPE overlapping 30-40 kt \ndeep layer bulk shear and 100-200 SRH, and additional severe storms \nwill be possible, for which SPC depicts a Marginal Risk. The pattern \nwill continue through Friday if not Saturday as well.\n\nOn Sunday, a cold front will traverse the area driven by a more \npotent shortwave moving through the northern states. Additional \nshowers and thunderstorms may accompany this frontal passage, then a \nquieter period should ensue for early next week.\n\nHighs will range from 80s to lower 90s, with dewpoints well into the \n70s, yielding afternoon heat index values approaching 105 daily for \nportions of central and southeast IL, especially southern portions. \nHowever, thunderstorm activity will greatly influence temperatures \nand heat index, and predictability of heat index remains low. Highs \nlook to reach only into the 80s for early next week.",
